   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:57,502:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: beta
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:57,502:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: _
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:57,502:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: 0 + N}$$
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:57,517:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: With uniform priors (α₀ = β₀ = 1), this starts at 0.5 (maximum uncertainty) and shifts towards 1.0 as positive evidence
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:57,522:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: accumulates or towards 0.0 as negative evidence dominates. The beauty of this formulation is that it naturally
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:57,523:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: handles uncertainty — a post with very few votes stays close to 0.5, reflecting genuine uncertainty about its veracity.
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:57,524:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: Variance Computation
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:57,524:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: We also compute the weighted variance of votes to assess the degree of community disagreement:
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:57,524:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: $$Var = 
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:57,525:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: \
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:57,525:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: frac{
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:57,525:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: \
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:57,526:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: sum
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:57,526:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: _
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:57,526:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: i w
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:57,527:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: _
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:57,527:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: i 
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:57,527:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: \
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:57,528:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: cdot (v
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:57,528:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: _
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:57,528:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: i - C
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:57,528:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: _
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:57,529:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: {Bayes})^2}{N}$$
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:57,544:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: High variance indicates a contentious post where users disagree about its veracity. The propagation decision engine
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:57,549:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: uses variance as one of its gating conditions — posts with high variance are not allowed to expand their reach even if
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:57,550:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: their mean credibility is above threshold, because the high disagreement suggests the assessment is not yet settled.
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:57,551:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: 32 — Department of Computer Science and Engineering, National Institute of Technology Srinagar
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:57,552:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: ML and Memory Augmentation
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:57,552:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: Starting from Phase 5, the Post Engine is augmented with two additional credibility signals:
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:57,553:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: ML Prediction (C
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:57,553:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: _
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:57,553:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: ML)
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:57,560:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: : A scikit-learn LogisticRegression model trained on features including the
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:57,563:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: variance-to-evidence ratio, user weight statistics, temporal spread, and anomaly concentration. The model is trained
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:57,564:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: on resolved posts and provides a supplementary credibility estimate.
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:57,564:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: Memory Score (C
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:57,564:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: _
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:57,565:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: memory)
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:57,574:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: : A TF-IDF based text similarity system that compares new posts against historically
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:57,578:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: resolved posts. If a new post is highly similar to a past post that was ultimately determined to be false, the memory
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:57,579:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: score biases credibility downward. This mechanism enables the system to 
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:57,579:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: "
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:57,579:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: remember
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:57,580:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: "
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:57,580:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment:  past misinformation
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:57,580:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: campaigns.
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:57,581:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: The final credibility combines all three signals:
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:57,581:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: $$C
