   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,233:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: 50 — Department of Computer Science and Engineering, National Institute of Technology Srinagar
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,234:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: <div style="page-break-after: always;"></div>
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,235:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: CHAPTER 4
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,235:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,243:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: This chapter presents the experimental evaluation of the NCPS across all six development phases. We describe the
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,246:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: evaluation metrics, simulation configuration, phase-wise results, frontend implementation, functional tests, and
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,247:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: stress test scenarios. All experiments were conducted using the system
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,247:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: '
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,248:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: s built-in simulation framework, which
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,249:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: models diverse user populations with configurable behaviors.
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,249:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: 4.1 Evaluation Metrics
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,250:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: We employ six quantitative metrics to assess system performance. Each metric captures a different aspect of the
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,251:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: system
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,251:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: '
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,251:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: s effectiveness.
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,252:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: 4.1.1 Accuracy
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,253:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: Accuracy measures the fraction of posts whose credibility assessment agrees with the ground truth label. A post is
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,253:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: classified as 
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,254:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: "
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,254:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: true
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,255:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: "
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,255:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment:  if its final credibility C
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,255:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: _
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,256:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: final ≥ 0.5 and 
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,256:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: "
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,256:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: false
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,257:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: "
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,257:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment:  otherwise:
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,258:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: $$Accuracy = 
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,258:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: \
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,258:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: frac{1}{|P|} 
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,259:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: \
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,259:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: sum
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,260:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: _
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,260:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: {j=1}^{|P|} 
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,261:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: \
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,261:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: mathbb{1}
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,261:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: [
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,262:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: \
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,262:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: text{sign}(C
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,262:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: _
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,263:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: j - 0.5) = y
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,263:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: _
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,264:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: j
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,264:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: ]
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,264:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: $$
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,265:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: where y
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,265:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: _
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,267:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: j ∈ {0, 1} is the ground truth label. This is the most intuitive metric — it simply tells us what fraction of posts the
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,267:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: system correctly identifies.
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,268:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: 4.1.2 Attack Success Rate
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,270:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: The attack success rate measures the fraction of false posts that the system fails to identify — i.e., false posts that end
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,271:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: up with credibility ≥ 0.5 despite being fabricated:
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,271:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: $$ASR = 
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,272:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: \
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,272:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: frac{|
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,273:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: {
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,273:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: j : y
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,273:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: _
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,274:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: j = 0 
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,274:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: \
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,275:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: wedge C
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,275:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: _
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,276:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: j 
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,276:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: \
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,277:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: geq 0.5
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,277:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: }
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,277:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: |}{|
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,278:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: {
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,278:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: j : y
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,279:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: _
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,279:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: j = 0
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,279:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: }
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,280:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: |}$$
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,281:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: A lower attack success rate is better. An ASR of 0 means that no false posts escaped detection, which is the ideal
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,281:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: outcome.
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,282:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: 4.1.3 Brier Score
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,282:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: The Brier score measures calibration — how well the system
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,283:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: '
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,283:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: s probability estimates match actual outcomes:
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,284:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: $$Brier = 
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,284:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: \
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,285:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: frac{1}{|P|} 
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,285:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: \
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,286:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: sum
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,286:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: _
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,287:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: {j=1}^{|P|} (C
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,287:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: _
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,287:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: j - y
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,288:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: _
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,288:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: j)^2$$
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,296:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: Unlike accuracy which is binary, the Brier score penalizes confident wrong predictions more heavily than uncertain
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,298:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: ones. A perfectly calibrated system would assign C=1.0 to all true posts and C=0.0 to all false posts, yielding a Brier
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,298:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: score of 0.
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,299:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: 4.1.4 Weight Correlation
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,299:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: Weight correlation measures how well the system
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,300:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: '
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,300:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: s estimated user weights correspond to users
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,300:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: '
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,304:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment:  actual reliabilities.
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,306:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: We compute the Pearson correlation coefficient between the vector of computed weights and the vector of true
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,306:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: reliability probabilities (known in simulation):
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,306:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: $$
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,307:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: \
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,307:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: rho = 
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,307:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: \
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,308:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: text{corr}(w, p
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,308:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: _
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,308:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: {true})$$
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,314:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: A higher weight correlation indicates that the system correctly identifies trustworthy users and assigns them higher
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,314:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: influence. This metric is particularly important because accurate weight estimation is a prerequisite for accurate
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,315:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: credibility estimation.
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,315:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: 4.1.5 Anomaly Precision
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,316:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: Anomaly precision measures the fraction of users flagged as anomalous (Anom > 0.3) that are truly malicious (bots
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,317:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: or adversarial):
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,317:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: $$AP = 
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,317:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: \
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,317:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: frac{TP}{TP + FP}$$
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,318:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: High precision means the system doesn
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,318:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: '
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,319:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: t falsely accuse legitimate users of being anomalous.
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,319:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: 4.1.6 Anomaly Recall
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,320:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: Anomaly recall measures the fraction of truly malicious users that the system successfully identifies:
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,320:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: $$AR = 
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,320:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: \
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,320:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: frac{TP}{TP + FN}$$
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,325:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: High recall means the system catches most of the malicious users. There is typically a tension between precision and
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,325:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: recall — our system achieves perfect precision (1.0) while steadily improving recall across phases.
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,326:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: 52 — Department of Computer Science and Engineering, National Institute of Technology Srinagar
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,327:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: 4.2 Simulation Configuration
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,329:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: All experiments use the following simulation configuration, designed to model a realistic local news scenario with
   DEBUG: 2026-06-01 02:19:58,330:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: diverse user types and adversarial behaviors:
